#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:5
#: dcedb992ee9543fcbcd17ba35220b4d9
msgid "Operation Using Only a Ground Control Station (GCS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:7
#: fc73e0c76064495ca0b2c1cc8eeda8aa
msgid "While it is generally recommended that a Radio Control system is employed as primary pilot control means for ArduPilot vehicles, it is not a requirement. It is possible to operate a vehicle using only a Ground Control Station (GCS), with or without joystick input. Setup varies vehicle to vehicle, and is detailed below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:11
#: ffd000c63ed84eb2a2c96274e1857d84
msgid "Capability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:13
#: b2ed0a4edd9d4f27be88f38aad3235bb
msgid "If a joystick is connected to the GCS, then almost the same control capabilities are available as with a radio control transmitter/receiver. Mode changes can be assigned to joystick buttons. If an RC transmitter is used as the joystick, then the same control precision can be obtained. Use of low resolution , cheaper \"game\" style joysticks will usually provide a less than enjoyable experience and use of high quality joystick or use of an RC transmitter as a joystick (OpenTX provides this capability) is highly recommended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:15
#: 6c160dad19714285905e70fbfe83394d
msgid "It is also possible to use only the GCS itself, if only autonomous missions or guided mode operation is desired."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:18
#: ce6f7be7d2134b73a0efa99d2367b6b9
msgid "Failsafes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:20
#: a8d081d55a7f40209e79dfd482165760
msgid "It is highly recommended that the GCS failsafe be setup for the vehicle, since GCS connectivity is being relied upon exclusively for vehicle control. See :ref:`apms-failsafe-function`, :ref:`Copter Failsafe <failsafe-landing-page>`, or :ref:`Rover Failsafes <rover-failsafes>`, as appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:22
#: 28a7e079ecfc44ceb7e75364fbc30f49
msgid "RC failsafe can also be used if a joystick is being used to send RC information (RC_OVERRIDES) via MAVLink. If the joystick fails or is disconnected, and normal :ref:`radio-failsafe` will occur."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:24
#: 588e5801c8584ef881cc95073e61a047
msgid "Of course, other failsafes can be setup and utilized as desired in addition to the GCS failsafe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:27
#: 44d9d5f58cf844e694f90299be9cb151
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:30
#: a7ee7b0dfe924da9bd4252ead4d9798f
msgid "Plane and Rover"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:32
#: 5a00d4d02bea4891836ddbd2c80fbe60
msgid "Both Plane and Rover only require two parameter changes to allow the arming of the vehicle when using only a GCS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:34
#: e66a4a175ddb48dc8f86d8b6c7aee0c1
msgid "If you are NOT using a joystick to provide RC overrides via MAVLink, or you do not want a failsafe if the joystick fails, then in Plane, :ref:`THR_FAILSAFE<THR_FAILSAFE>` must be set to zero, while in Rover, :ref:`FS_THR_ENABLE<FS_THR_ENABLE>` must be set to zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:35
#: e61d9fdb73664a87bc8187cfdb87b120
msgid "In both Plane and Rover, :ref:`RC_PROTOCOLs<RC_PROTOCOLs>` should be set to \"0\" to prevent accidental detection of noise as an RC source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:37
#: 83b66ff208154c4e8d689b144eee1fe8
msgid "The vehicle can then be armed after the GPS has obtained lock and internal initializations have been completed, either by joystick rudder arming (if enabled, see :ref:`ARMING_RUDDER<ARMING_RUDDER>` ) or arming command from the GCS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:41
#: 63530286cc1d4a18add8bfeee8d8edef
msgid "Copter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:43
#: 06b9bd93825f4a1b9c02310d07d74f43
msgid "Copter has a few additional safety checks that must be accommodated in order to arm with no RC input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:45
#: 89cf7af996d64f22a82f9e01361dc34c
msgid ":ref:`FS_THR_ENABLE<FS_THR_ENABLE>` must be set to zero if no joystick is being used or you do not want a failsafe if the joystick fails or is disabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:46
#: 214da1c3667c401ba2460c600e76cd24
msgid ":ref:`ARMING_SKIPCHK<ARMING_SKIPCHK>` should include bit 6 (RC check). It is not recommended to turn on other bits since important system checks may be bypassed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:47
#: 85c88dd004b24ce1b3657e0e20731978
msgid "assuming that the :ref:`RCMAP_ROLL <RCMAP_ROLL>`, :ref:`RCMAP_PITCH <RCMAP_PITCH>`, :ref:`RCMAP_THROTTLE <RCMAP_THROTTLE>` and :ref:`RCMAP_YAW <RCMAP_YAW>` parameters are default, then the RC 1 thru 4 channels' maximum and minimum parameters must be changed slightly. This is because Copter checks to see that the RC calibration has been done. But since no RC is used in the system, this step will not have occurred. Therefore, to emulate that this has happened, the min and max values need to be changed from their defaults of 1100 and 1900, respectively. Values of 1101 and 1901, will do for the ``RCx_MIN`` and ``RCx_MAX`` for the roll,pitch, throttle, and yaw RC channels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:49
#: 1ccd0a042e784b38aeea11c56b42d40d
msgid "The vehicle can then be armed after the GPS has obtained lock and internal initializations have completed, either by joystick rudder arming (if enabled, see :ref:`ARMING_RUDDER<ARMING_RUDDER>` ) or arming command from the GCS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gcs-only-operation.rst:51
#: ff2144f92eba486fbc1b885264f7333e
msgid "However, there is still one more safety check that needs to be cleared. Copter will auto-disarm after a few seconds if the throttle is not raised above idle. If a joystick is used, this is easily accomplished. However, if AUTO or GUIDED operation without a joystick is desired, then after arming, a MISSION START command must be issued in AUTO mode, as shown below for Mission Planner, or a TAKEOFF command be issued from the GCS (in Mission Planner, this is a mouse right button click option) within a few seconds of arming. QGC has a slider button that will arm and start the mission in one swipe."
msgstr ""
